URL: https://developer.pff.com/openapi.json\nSTATUS: 200\n\n{
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "info": {
    "title": "PFF Premium Stats Pro CLI API",
    "version": "2.1.0",
    "summary": "Every operation the pff-pro CLI wraps: the original Premium Stats Pro API, mirrored under /v1, and PFF's own contract under /v2.",
    "description": "The machine-readable contract for the `pff-pro` CLI, served public-read at `GET /openapi.json`\nand `GET /openapi.yaml`. It describes the 59 read-only operations of the original Premium Stats Pro API\nunder `/v1` — league and game reference data, team and player reports, league-wide leaderboards and\nPFF signature stats — and PFF's own operations under `/v2`, starting with the team page.\n\n**Two surfaces, one document.** `servers[].url` is the bare origin, and every path key carries\nits own version prefix. `/v1/...` mirrors the original Premium Stats Pro JSON API exactly — snake_case,\nwire format untouched, the same reports the website has served for years. `/v2/...` is PFF's own\ncontract over the newer data — camelCase, a `columns` + `rows` table shape, and later a CSV form of\nevery table; `team-stats` is its first operation, the rest of the team page follows. Command names\ncome from `operationId`, so which surface a command reaches is not something a caller ever has to\nthink about.\n\n**Every operation also answers `HEAD`**, with the same status and headers and no body.\n\n**No response is cacheable, with exactly one exception.** Every operation below carries\n`cache-control: no-store`. The exception is not an operation at all: this document, served at\n`/openapi.json` and `/openapi.yaml`, carries `cache-control: public, max-age=3600` and a\nstrong `ETag`, so a CLI can revalidate it with `If-None-Match` and get a `304`.\n\n**Every response carries `x-request-id`** — the caller's own `x-request-id` when it sent a\nwell-formed one, and a fresh UUID otherwise. It is repeated in `error.request_id` on every error,\nand it is the one thing worth quoting in a bug report.\n\n**No `Link` headers.** This API never emits `Link: rel=\"next\"`, so a client that auto-paginates on\nit (restish among them) has nothing to follow. Result-set breadth is controlled by the query\nparameters.\n\n**Errors are one shape, always JSON.** Every failure below is\n`{\"error\":{\"code\",\"message\",\"request_id\",\"details\"?}}`, and `error.code` is the field to branch on —\nit is stable, and the human-readable `message` is not. Stable means an existing code never changes its\nmeaning; the table below can still GROW (`rate_limited` arrived in 2.1.0), so a client that switches on\n`error.code` needs a default branch — treat an unknown code by its HTTP status.\n\n| Status | `error.code` | Means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `400` | `invalid_parameter` | A required parameter is missing or empty, or the parameters were rejected. |\n| `401` | `unauthorized` | The credential is missing or was refused; `details.reason` says which. |\n| `403` | `forbidden` | The credential verified but may not do this — see `details.reason` below. |\n| `404` | `not_found` | No such route, or no such report. |\n| `422` | `upstream_error` | The export spans too many weeks or seasons. |\n| `429` | `rate_limited` / `upstream_error` | A budget is exhausted — this API's own per-account one, or the data source's export metering; `Retry-After` says when to come back. |\n| `500` | `internal_error` | This API failed. |\n| `502` | `upstream_error` | The data behind this API failed, or could not be reached. |\n| `503` | `upstream_error` / `session_provisioning` | Temporarily unavailable; `Retry-After` says when to come back. |\n| `504` | `upstream_timeout` | The request did not complete in time. |\n\n**One command per operation.** `operationId` is the command name. The taxonomy is flat\nkebab-case with a family prefix — `ref-`, `team-`, `player-`, `facet-`, `signature-` — because\nthere are no nested command groups to hang a tree off; ten of the commands also answer to a short\nalias (`leagues`, `games`, `players`, `teams`, `passing`, `receiving`, `rushing`, `defense`,\n`blocking`, `special-teams`). A **required** parameter becomes a positional argument, in the order\nthe operation declares it; every other parameter is a `--kebab-case` flag. The `facet` leaderboards\nand `ref-leagues` require nothing, so every filter on them is a flag. The rest take positionals:\n`ref-players` and `team-list` take `league`; `team-overview` takes `league season`; `ref-games`\nand the four `signature` commands take `league season week`; `team-summary` takes\n`league season franchise-id`; the `player-` reports take `league player-id`, and seven of them\n(`player-passing-concept`, `player-passing-depth`, `player-passing-pressure`,\n`player-receiving-depth`, `player-rushing-direction`, `player-snaps-summary`,\n`player-position-pivot`) take `league season player-id`. Each command's `--help` prints its order.\n\n**Budgets.** Every account has a read budget and an export budget, each per minute, shared across\nevery operation here and across both credentials the account may hold; a `/v1` request with\n`export=true` on an operation that honours it, or a `/v2` request with `format=csv`, spends an\nexport slot, and anything else spends a read. Every counted response — refused or not — carries\n`x-ratelimit-limit`, `x-ratelimit-remaining` and `x-ratelimit-reset` (seconds since the Unix\nepoch), so a script can slow down before it is told to; over budget is a `429` with\n`error.code` `rate_limited`, `error.details.scope` naming the budget, and a `Retry-After`.\n`whoami` and `logout` are never counted.\n\n**Retries are the caller's business, and none is a reasonable choice.** Every operation here is a\nread, so retrying one is safe — but an export spends its quota whether or not the attempt\nsucceeds, and this API never sends `Retry-After` on anything a retry would fix except `429` and\n`503`. A restish-based client retries twice by default; `RSH_RETRY=0` in the environment, or\n`--rsh-retry 0` on the command, makes every command a single attempt.\n\n**Authentication.** Every operation below needs one of two credentials, described fully under\n`securitySchemes`: `pffOAuth` — sign in with your PFF account in the browser, once; refresh\nhappens silently afterwards — or `pffApiKey` — a PFF API key, for scripts and CI, created at\nwww.pff.com/account/api-keys (the API-keys section of your pff.com account page). `x-cli-config`\npre-seeds the `pffOAuth` browser flow so `restish api connect` sets it up with no further input; a\nsecond, API-key profile is a client's own to add with `restish api set` (`api configure` was the\nrestish v1 command). `whoami` reports what this API believes about the caller without spending\nanything; `logout` ends this host's access for the credential presented.\n\n**CSV.** 33 of the 59 operations take `?export=true` and answer `200 text/csv` with a\n`Content-Disposition` attachment instead of JSON; on the other 26 the parameter is ignored and is\nnot declared. Ask for `Accept: application/json` even when exporting — `Accept: text/csv` is\nrefused with a `406`, and the CSV content type is set on the response regardless. An **unentitled**\ncaller gets a `200 text/csv` with an empty body rather than a `403`; see `CsvBody`.\n\n**CSV on `/v2`.** Every `/v2` table operation takes `format=csv` (`--format csv`) and answers\n`200 text/csv` with a `Content-Disposition` attachment — the same table as its JSON body, header row\nof column keys, CRLF lines, RFC 4180 quoting; see `CsvTable`. `Accept` is not consulted: one\nmechanism, stated here. Errors keep the JSON envelope whatever `format` asked for.\n\n**Report columns are data-driven.** A report's stat columns are not enumerated here — which ones come\nback depends on the report, the position, the query and the entitlement — so the schemas below pin\nthe envelope and the identity columns exactly and leave the rest to `additionalProperties`.",
